        //- Part 2: Technical Challenge (~16 min)
        //- Brief: explain the context of the challenge, the steps taken to address it, and the
        //- outcome. Highlight the problem-solving approach and the skills used to overcome it.
        //- The stubs assume default-deny egress plus an allow list for the release path. Rework
        //- them to match what was actually built. The names, label, and address in the two
        //- example policies are placeholders.
        section
          section#challenge
            h2 Technical Challenge
            h3 Controlling Message Release from a Kubernetes Cluster
            ul
              li
                a(href='#/context') Context
              li
                a(href='#/approach') Approach
              li
                a(href='#/outcome') Outcome
          section#context
            h2 Context
            ul
              li MDPAP is an operational missile detection, tracking, and warning system that is currently in use by the US Space Force.
              li In an operational MDPAP system, data can be played back or simulated to test the system or review a previous event to compare it to what is currently happening.
              li We needed to ensure that messages that were only in an `operations` state and were marked correctly would be released from the cluster to end users throughout the world.

            p 
            p Takeaway - 3 conditions needed to be met for a message release: correct instance state, simulation tag, and playback tag.

          section
            h2 The Challenge
            ul
              li There are MANY messages flying around the system and we have very tight latency requirements.
              li If a bad message were to leave a system, there are many downstream consequences.
              li The legacy system used adaptors that unpacked every message in every cluster to match the release requirements.
          section
            h2 Solution Space - Kubernetes Network Policies
            ul
              li By default pods are not isolated: all traffic in and out is allowed but a NetworkPolicy selects pods by label and lists the ingress or egress they are allowed
              li Once selected, a pod only gets the traffic a policy allows in that direction.
              li The CNI plugin enforces policies, so they do nothing without one that supports them.
            ul 
              li By implementing Network Policies we could check initial conditions for state without unpacking the messages which saved a lot of processing time and latency in the system.
          section#approach
            h2 Approach
            ul
              li We implemented message gateways that are basically proxies which have external connections.
              li The gateways would check the data packets for simulation and playback data on arrival.
              li A Kubernetes operator and CRD allowed us to control this all based on state of the cluster
          section
            h2 Start From Default Deny
            ul 
              li Network policies are fairly easy to implement, but default denying everything cuts off all communication to the pod
            pre
              code.language-yaml(data-trim data-line-numbers)
                | apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
                | kind: NetworkPolicy
                | metadata:
                |   name: default-deny-egress
                |   namespace: communications
                | spec:
                |   podSelector: {annotations...}
                |   policyTypes:
                |     - Egress
          section
            h2 Allow the Release Path
            pre
              code.language-yaml(data-trim data-line-numbers)
                | apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
                | kind: NetworkPolicy
                | metadata:
                |   name: allow-message-release
                | spec:
                |   podSelector:
                |     matchLabels:
                |       app: message-release
                |   policyTypes:
                |     - Egress
                |   egress:
                |     - to:
                |         - ipBlock:
                |             ranges
                |       ports:
                |         - protocol: TCP
                |           port: port
            p Once the conditions are met, the CRD will allow the pod to communicate via the policy.
          section#outcome
            h2 Outcome
            ul
              li This allowed us to reclaim ~10% of CPU power in the system to be used elsewhere.
              li Ensured that we met our release requirements at a cluster level, even if we had code that tried to release messages, it was not allowed.
              li Communication Pods had to be restarted to ensure that long lasting connections like sockets are terminated.
          section
            h2 Problem Solving and Skills
            ul
              li The problem was easier solved by breaking out the state into the cluster level and the data tagging at the message level.
              li By removing one of the problems and solving for that condition first, efficiencies can be gained.
              li Future work:
              ul 
                li Consider breaking out metadata from the message to make it quicker to unmarshal.
                li Hot load the pods so that they do not need to be restarted.
