          section
            h2 The Team
            ul
              li The team is 11 engineers spread across multiple timezones and 3 companies.
              li Each area and company had their own culture and way of working.
              li I needed the team to be cohesive, with active participation from everyone to get the best product.
          section#whiteboarding
            h2 Whiteboarding Sessions
            ul
              li Ownership is key to long term success.
              li Allowing the engineers to come up with key pieces of the design gives them that agency that is needed for real ownership.
              li Whiteboarding sessions allow the engineers to come together and discuss possibilities and dig into the design, rather than be told to implement something
              li It also allows for communication that is technical which can help form the basis of a real bond between people.
          section#totem
            h2 The "Thank You" or "Great Work" Totem
            ul
              li Recognition is a key piece to motivating people, but it doesn't have to be complicated or expensive.
              li Expressing gratitude to others on the team helps form a better bond and builds good habits and bonds.
              li A totem (in this case, a simple clay wizard) is passed to another person during a scrum at least once a sprint with the giver saying why the next person deserved it.
              li These could be small things, or major things. The idea is to just get people to interact more in a positive way.
            p.todo TODO: Photo of the totem
          section#shoutouts
            h2 Team Shoutouts
            ul
              li A simple kind word can motivate people to move mountains, and positive reinforcement is often lacking from the workforce.
              li An email or acknowledgement during a meeting can mean the world to some people.
          section
            h2 What Changed
